\section*{Alignment-Based Information Modelling (AIM)}

Railway alignments are traditionally described as geometric curves in
space.  
This perspective is insufficient for modern engineering tasks, which
require the integration of design, measurement, construction, and
optimization.

\textbf{Alignment is not a curve.}  
It is a curvature-driven system whose geometry emerges from a structured
process.

This work introduces \emph{Alignment-Based Information Modelling (AIM)}
as a unified framework for this perspective.

---

\section*{Core Idea}

AIM represents alignments intrinsically by curvature:
\[
\kappa(s).
\]

This formulation:
\begin{itemize}
\item unifies geometry and dynamics,
\item reduces model complexity,
\item enables efficient computation and optimization.
\end{itemize}

---

\section*{From Representation to Process}

AIM replaces static modelling by an operator pipeline:
\[
\mathcal{P}
=
\mathcal{W}^{-1}
\circ
\mathcal{R}
\circ
\mathcal{M}
\circ
\mathcal{W}
\circ
\mathcal{C}.
\]

This pipeline connects:
\begin{itemize}
\item coordinate systems,
\item world geometry,
\item intrinsic track coordinates,
\item modelling,
\item realization,
\item and optimization.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Alignment modelling becomes a process.}

---

\section*{Hybrid Modelling}

AIM combines structure and flexibility:
\[
\kappa(s)
=
\kappa_{\mathrm{param}}(s;\mathbf{T})
+
\delta\kappa(s).
\]

This hybrid representation:
\begin{itemize}
\item preserves engineering intent,
\item integrates measurement data,
\item avoids overfitting.
\end{itemize}

---

\section*{Realization as a First-Class Concept}

The designed alignment is not identical to the constructed track.

A realization operator
\[
\mathcal{R}
\]
maps the theoretical model to physical geometry, introducing smoothing
and constraints.

\textbf{Design must therefore be performed in realized space.}

---

\section*{Optimization}

Alignment design is formulated as a structured nonlinear least-squares
problem.

Due to:
\begin{itemize}
\item locality in arc-length,
\item sparse Jacobians,
\item intrinsic block structure,
\end{itemize}
the problem is efficiently solvable using SQP methods.

---

\section*{World-to-Track Mapping}

A central component is the transformation:
\[
x \leftrightarrow (s,d).
\]

This mapping:
\begin{itemize}
\item links measurement data to the model,
\item introduces nonlinear complexity,
\item motivates hybrid and LOD-based strategies.
\end{itemize}

---

\section*{Key Principles}

\begin{itemize}
\item curvature-centric modelling,
\item separation of model and realization,
\item hybrid parametric--discrete representation,
\item operator-based architecture,
\item optimization in realized space,
\item exploitation of sparsity and structure.
\end{itemize}

---

\section*{Outcome}

AIM provides a coherent framework for:
\begin{itemize}
\item reconstruction from imperfect data,
\item physically meaningful optimization,
\item integration of design, measurement, and construction,
\item scalable computational implementation.
\end{itemize}

---

\section*{Conclusion}

Alignment modelling is not a purely geometric task.

It is the integration of:
\begin{itemize}
\item geometry,
\item physics,
\item data,
\item and computation.
\end{itemize}

AIM formalizes this integration as a structured operator framework,
establishing a new foundation for railway alignment engineering.
